<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I set a deadline on a Traverse model call?"
  description="Pass timeout_ms on traverse-embedder-web@0.17.1 or on the traverse-runtime 0.17.1 model.execute payload. The host uses the smaller of that value and the manifest max_execution_ms."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-set-a-deadline-on-a-traverse-model-call.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> pass <code>timeout_ms</code> to <code>ExactModelBrowserHost.execute</code> on npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.17.1</code>, or set <code>timeout_ms</code> on the <code>model.execute</code> payload for <code>ExactModelHostConnector</code> in crates.io <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.1. The host uses the smaller of <code>timeout_ms</code> and the package manifest <code>max_execution_ms</code>. On a browser Worker and on wasmtime, a deadline that lands during the call stops the guest and the call fails with <code>timeout</code>. The walk-through is <a href="/blog/stop-a-model-call-mid-run.html">Stop a model.execute call while it is still running</a>.</p>

  <h2>Browser</h2>
  <p><code>host</code> is an <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code>. The other fields are the pin, the staged input, and the policy the package was registered under.</p>
  <pre><code>{browserSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>The deadline is measured from the start of the guest call. When it lands during a Worker call, the host terminates that Worker and throws <code>timeout</code> with message <code>model.execute exceeded timeout mid-run</code>. The package snapshot is dropped. A deadline that has already passed fails before the guest starts.</p>

  <h2>Native wasmtime</h2>
  <p>Put the same field on the <code>model.execute</code> payload:</p>
  <pre><code>{payloadSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>A watcher thread waits for that deadline and bumps the wasmtime epoch. The guest traps at its next epoch check. The call fails with <code>timeout</code> and message <code>model.execute exceeded timeout mid-run</code>. The same deadline covers <code>model_prepare</code>. An interrupted prepare keeps no snapshot. The watcher re-reads a separate cancel flag every 5 milliseconds. Cancellation is <a href="/questions/how-do-i-cancel-a-running-model-execute-call-in-traverse.html">its own call</a>.</p>

  <h2>Node, and a guest that never returns</h2>
  <p>Node has no <code>Worker</code>. <code>createWorker: null</code> is the same path. The host checks the deadline before the call and again after the guest returns. If the guest returns late, the host discards the output and fails with <code>timeout</code>. A guest that never returns stays running. The deadline does not unblock that call.</p>

  <h2>Registration</h2>
  <p><code>registerPackage</code> takes <code>preparationBudgetMs</code>, capped by <code>maxPreparationMs</code> (default five minutes). A placement that exceeds the budget is skipped. Passing a <code>signal</code> and aborting it fails registration with <code>cancelled</code> and keeps nothing. Package size, declared memory, and declared fuel are separate ceilings: over them, registration fails with <code>model_incompatible</code> and reason <code>host_limit_exceeded</code>. The browser checks <code>maxFuel</code> at registration. It does not meter fuel during the call.</p>

  <p>The rules are in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/exact-ref-model-execution.md">docs/exact-ref-model-execution.md</a> at tag v0.17.1 (Decisions 104 and 131, Spec 138 0.16.1 and 0.16.2, <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1582">traverse#1582</a>). Pin 0.17.1. crates.io has no <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.0. No host is Certified under Spec 529.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
